WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.680
All right everyone.

00:04.680 --> 00:06.600
So now we're going to talk about the variables.

00:06.600 --> 00:07.840
So what is a variable.

00:07.840 --> 00:13.600
It is like a placeholder where you store inside it a value in order to use it later inside your stored

00:13.600 --> 00:14.360
procedure.

00:14.360 --> 00:18.040
So that means variable holds like a value inside the memory.

00:18.040 --> 00:21.560
And you can reuse it everywhere you want inside your stored procedure.

00:21.560 --> 00:23.280
But it's not like the parameters.

00:23.280 --> 00:26.400
Parameters are something like outside the stored procedure.

00:26.400 --> 00:31.600
It's an input from the one that is executing the stored procedure, and the stored procedure has to

00:31.600 --> 00:33.520
adapt with the parameter.

00:33.520 --> 00:38.560
But a variable is something that lives inside the stored procedure, and we use it as a developers in

00:38.560 --> 00:42.640
order to make our code dynamic and to move a value from one place to another.

00:42.640 --> 00:44.200
So let's have a very simple example.

00:44.240 --> 00:49.040
Now, let's say that we don't want our report here about the total customers as a query.

00:49.040 --> 00:51.560
So I don't want it as a result in the output.

00:51.600 --> 00:54.040
Let's say I'm generating a report always like this.

00:54.040 --> 00:58.800
We are saying the total customers from Germany equal to two, and the average score from Germany is

00:58.800 --> 01:00.600
equal to 425.

01:00.640 --> 01:03.320
So I need it as a text, not as a table like here.

01:03.320 --> 01:09.450
So in order to do that we can use the T-sql SQL prints in order to give a message after executing the

01:09.450 --> 01:10.370
stored procedure.

01:10.370 --> 01:12.330
So the syntax of print is very simple.

01:12.330 --> 01:15.090
So we can go over here and say print.

01:15.130 --> 01:17.050
And then we have single quotes.

01:17.090 --> 01:22.850
And let's go and get the whole message from here without the comments and then the semicolon.

01:22.850 --> 01:26.090
And we can repeat that for the second message.

01:26.810 --> 01:30.970
So for the average score and we put it over here as well.

01:31.050 --> 01:31.810
Semicolon.

01:32.770 --> 01:35.970
Now if you do it like this this message can be always static.

01:35.970 --> 01:39.810
So we will have always like two for the total customers.

01:39.850 --> 01:44.010
And the average score can always be like this, even though that the data is changing.

01:44.010 --> 01:46.010
So we cannot have it static like this.

01:46.050 --> 01:50.330
We have to make it dynamic, and especially if we are calling this function for USA.

01:50.450 --> 01:52.250
So we cannot have it here as a Germany.

01:52.250 --> 01:54.250
So let's see how we can make this dynamic.

01:54.250 --> 01:55.850
Now let's start with the easy stuff.

01:55.970 --> 01:59.690
Instead of the Germany over here we can go and put our parameter right.

01:59.890 --> 02:01.330
So instead of this.

02:01.330 --> 02:03.850
So we're going to say ads country.

02:04.010 --> 02:06.970
But now the problem is it is part of the whole string.

02:06.970 --> 02:07.850
We cannot do that.

02:07.850 --> 02:09.690
So we're going to stop the text.

02:09.730 --> 02:14.700
And now we can see the coloring is changing and then have a plus in order to have concatenations.

02:14.700 --> 02:20.620
So this text comes first, then the value from the country, and then we can have as well the double

02:20.620 --> 02:23.940
point as static text and again a concatenation.

02:23.940 --> 02:26.020
And then we have the two we can talk about later.

02:26.020 --> 02:28.060
So let's do the same stuff over here.

02:28.060 --> 02:33.140
So we're going to say plus art country coloring is not changing because of this code.

02:33.140 --> 02:34.460
So let me just remove it.

02:34.460 --> 02:40.540
And then afterward a plus make it static again plus and remove the final quotes.

02:40.540 --> 02:45.940
So with that in the message we have now dynamic where we get the value of the country from the parameter.

02:45.940 --> 02:47.700
And now we come to the interesting part.

02:47.740 --> 02:48.780
We have here an issue.

02:49.060 --> 02:52.100
Those two values, they come from this query.

02:52.100 --> 02:54.260
And of course we cannot use a parameter for that.

02:54.300 --> 02:56.420
We have to use now the variables.

02:56.460 --> 02:59.300
Now in order to make the variables we have three steps.

02:59.300 --> 03:03.180
The first step is that we have to tell SQL about our new variable.

03:03.180 --> 03:07.020
So SQL can prepare and make like placeholder for it in the memory.

03:07.020 --> 03:10.460
So we have to tell SQL and prepare it with our new variables.

03:10.500 --> 03:16.180
Now usually we do all the declarations of our variables at the start of the stored procedure immediately

03:16.180 --> 03:17.150
after begin.

03:17.150 --> 03:20.310
So that means we're going to go over here and say declare.

03:21.030 --> 03:23.070
And now after that it's like the parameters.

03:23.070 --> 03:23.710
It's very simple.

03:23.710 --> 03:26.350
So add total customers.

03:26.350 --> 03:28.070
So this is the name of the variable.

03:28.070 --> 03:30.190
And after that we have to define the data type.

03:30.230 --> 03:33.030
Of course you have to understand the data type from the query.

03:33.070 --> 03:37.070
Since we are saying count star then the output can be an integer.

03:37.070 --> 03:38.710
That's why we're going to write it like this.

03:38.710 --> 03:39.430
So integer.

03:39.430 --> 03:42.230
And now we need another one for the average.

03:42.230 --> 03:44.190
So what we're going to do we can make a comma.

03:44.230 --> 03:46.470
Now we are declaring another variable.

03:46.470 --> 03:48.710
So add average score.

03:48.830 --> 03:52.550
And the data type of this one is going to be float because we have an average.

03:52.750 --> 03:53.390
So that's it.

03:53.390 --> 03:58.350
For the first step we are telling SQL we have two variables and SQL going to go and create an empty

03:58.350 --> 03:59.150
placeholder.

03:59.190 --> 04:03.430
So now in the second step we have to give our variables a value.

04:03.430 --> 04:04.750
So where are we going to get the values.

04:04.750 --> 04:06.470
We're going to get it from the query.

04:06.470 --> 04:07.590
So let's do that.

04:07.630 --> 04:08.830
Now let's start with the first column.

04:08.830 --> 04:10.790
As you can see we have here the count star.

04:10.790 --> 04:15.350
And as we learned anything that we write on the right side, it's going to be like an alias for the

04:15.350 --> 04:15.830
column.

04:15.830 --> 04:21.270
But in SQL if you go and write something before it, it's going to be the variable.

04:21.270 --> 04:26.320
So we can do it like this at total customers and then equal.

04:26.320 --> 04:32.120
So now we are saying whatever value this query returns, it should be stored inside my new variable

04:32.120 --> 04:35.240
so that I'm assigning values to my variable.

04:35.280 --> 04:41.200
But here there is one thing that we cannot have any more aliases because our query will not return any

04:41.200 --> 04:41.760
results.

04:41.800 --> 04:45.840
Our query have now only one task to assign values to my variables.

04:45.840 --> 04:47.480
So that's why we cannot have it like this.

04:47.480 --> 04:49.440
We have to remove the alias and the same thing.

04:49.440 --> 04:51.440
We're going to do it for the average.

04:51.440 --> 04:55.680
So add average score equal to the average score.

04:55.680 --> 04:57.200
And we have to remove the alias.

04:57.200 --> 04:58.000
So that's it.

04:58.040 --> 05:00.280
Now our query having different purpose.

05:00.480 --> 05:02.280
It is not for returning result.

05:02.280 --> 05:05.680
It is to assign values to our variables.

05:05.680 --> 05:06.760
So now we have values.

05:06.760 --> 05:08.720
In the next step we have to go and use it.

05:08.720 --> 05:12.240
And we can use our variables everywhere inside our stored procedure.

05:12.240 --> 05:13.360
So it could be in the print.

05:13.360 --> 05:15.000
It could be in the next query.

05:15.000 --> 05:20.600
So in any Select statement in any place, sometimes we use variables in order to pass an information

05:20.600 --> 05:22.680
from one query to another one.

05:22.680 --> 05:26.520
But in this example we want to use our variables inside the prints.

05:26.520 --> 05:27.520
So it is very simple.

05:27.770 --> 05:30.690
Now we're going to go and replace the static number.

05:30.690 --> 05:34.850
And it's like the parameter we're going to say at total customers.

05:34.970 --> 05:39.690
And the same thing for the average at average score.

05:39.810 --> 05:40.570
So that's it.

05:40.570 --> 05:41.330
It's very simple.

05:41.450 --> 05:45.170
So again the step one we have to declare them to define it for SQL.

05:45.170 --> 05:47.130
And with that we're going to get an empty variable.

05:47.250 --> 05:50.770
The second step we have to add values to those variables.

05:50.770 --> 05:53.570
And the last step we have to go and use those variables.

05:53.570 --> 05:59.050
So it makes sense right now if you check our message over here you can see that everything is dynamic.

05:59.050 --> 06:00.930
And we don't have any static values.

06:00.930 --> 06:03.410
But there is one more thing that's in the print.

06:03.450 --> 06:05.450
Everything should be as a string.

06:05.450 --> 06:08.530
So we cannot have dates, numbers, floats and so on.

06:08.530 --> 06:11.770
So that's why you have to make check if you are adding any parameter and variables.

06:11.770 --> 06:13.610
All of them should be string.

06:13.610 --> 06:19.890
So the country it is okay because we have the data type of varchar, but the total number and the average

06:19.890 --> 06:20.370
score.

06:20.570 --> 06:23.530
This is not really good because they have different data type.

06:23.530 --> 06:27.730
And we have to go and now cast those data types to another one.

06:27.730 --> 06:33.580
So we're going to say cast and we're going to say here as and varchar so that we don't get any errors

06:33.580 --> 06:34.340
from SQL.

06:34.380 --> 06:40.140
So cast as well here as Nvarchar like this.

06:40.180 --> 06:40.660
All right.

06:40.660 --> 06:41.940
So I think we are ready.

06:42.100 --> 06:46.300
Let's go and change the definition of our stored procedure in order to test.

06:46.300 --> 06:47.700
So let's go and execute.

06:47.740 --> 06:48.340
Perfect.

06:48.340 --> 06:49.820
And now let's go and test.

06:49.820 --> 06:53.820
So let's start with the defaults where we have the parameter as USA.

06:53.900 --> 06:56.140
So now as you can see we are getting one result.

06:56.140 --> 06:57.900
And this is from the second query.

06:57.900 --> 07:01.540
So the first query is not returning anything anymore in the output.

07:01.580 --> 07:05.420
But if you go to the messages over here you can see we have a new message.

07:05.420 --> 07:13.660
It says total customers from USA is equal to three and the average score from USA is equal to 825.

07:13.660 --> 07:16.300
And this is exactly what we wanted for our reports.

07:16.340 --> 07:19.980
Now let's go and execute the parameter equal to Germany.

07:20.020 --> 07:21.620
Again we have only one result.

07:21.620 --> 07:25.860
And in the messages we're going to get total customers from Germany is equal to two.

07:25.900 --> 07:29.820
And the average score from Germany is equal to 425.

07:29.820 --> 07:31.860
So this is exactly how we work with the variables.

07:31.860 --> 07:37.820
We use it in order to hold one information in one place, in order to reuse it later in different place.

07:37.820 --> 07:39.180
So that's it for variables.
